--  Time: a monotonic clock and sleeping on it, and a wall clock, two
--  concepts beside core/io's and outside `world`, so a provider without
--  a clock, and a firmware image that never asks for the time, carries
--  none of them.  Time is counted in nanoseconds in a `u64`: no floating
--  point, the same arithmetic on every target, and room for 584 years.

duration_value: type = distinct u64
instant_value: type = distinct u64

--- A span of time, a whole number of nanoseconds from zero to 2^64 - 1,
--- about 584 years.  `u64(span)` is the count and `io.duration(n)` makes
--- one, as `nanoseconds` does; `milliseconds` and the others make one from
--- a coarser count and trap when it would not fit.  Two spans compare with
--- `==`, `<>`, `<`, `<=`, `>` and `>=`, as their counts do; the type has
--- no arithmetic of its own, so an instant is never added to an instant.
public duration: type = duration_value

--- A reading of a monotonic clock: nanoseconds since a start the provider
--- fixes, which is the system's boot on Linux.  Only the difference
--- between two readings of one provider means anything, `elapsed`; the
--- count is `u64(at)`.  Two instants compare with `==`, `<>`, `<`, `<=`,
--- `>` and `>=`, as their counts do.
public instant: type = instant_value

--- A reading of the wall clock: UTC seconds since 1970-01-01T00:00:00,
--- negative before it, and the nanoseconds after them, from 0 to
--- 999,999,999.  Leap seconds are not counted, as POSIX counts none.
--- Calendars, time zones and formatting a date are not this library's.
public wall_time: type = struct
    seconds: i64
    nanoseconds: u32
end wall_time

--- A monotonic clock and sleeping on it.  `now` never goes back: a later
--- reading of one provider is never less than an earlier one, whatever
--- the wall clock does.  `sleep` returns once at least `span` has passed
--- on this clock, measured from when it was called; it may return later,
--- as late as the system schedules it, and never sooner.
public clock: type = concept (provider: type)
    --- The clock's reading now.
    now: (self: provider) -> (at: instant)
    --- Wait until at least `span` has passed on this clock.
    sleep: (self: provider, span: duration) -> none
end clock

--- The wall clock: what time it is in UTC, as the system keeps it.  It is
--- a concept of its own because a firmware image may count time without
--- knowing the date, and because the system may set this clock, so two
--- readings may go back; time a task with `clock`, never with this.
public wall_clock: type = concept (provider: type)
    --- The wall clock's reading now.
    wall_now: (self: provider) -> (at: wall_time)
end wall_clock

--- The reading of a monotonic clock now, through `clock` evidence.
public now: (provider: type is clock, host: provider) -> (at: instant) =
    at = provider.now(host)
end now

--- Wait until at least `span` has passed on the provider's monotonic
--- clock.  A sleep a signal interrupts on a hosted system sleeps on for
--- what is left; a sleep of zero returns at once.
public sleep: (provider: type is clock, host: provider, span: duration)
              -> none =
    provider.sleep(host, span)
end sleep

--- The wall clock's reading now, through `wall_clock` evidence.
public wall_now: (provider: type is wall_clock, host: provider)
                 -> (at: wall_time) =
    at = provider.wall_now(host)
end wall_now

--- A span of `count` nanoseconds.
public nanoseconds: (count: u64) -> (span: duration) =
    span = duration_value(count)
end nanoseconds

--- A span of `count` microseconds; it traps beyond 584 years.
public microseconds: (count: u64) -> (span: duration) =
    span = duration_value(count * 1000)
end microseconds

--- A span of `count` milliseconds; it traps beyond 584 years.
public milliseconds: (count: u64) -> (span: duration) =
    span = duration_value(count * 1000000)
end milliseconds

--- A span of `count` seconds; it traps beyond 584 years.
public seconds: (count: u64) -> (span: duration) =
    span = duration_value(count * 1000000000)
end seconds

--- The span from `earlier` to `later`, two readings of one clock taken in
--- that order.  It traps when `later` is the earlier of the two, which
--- two readings of one monotonic clock taken in order never are.
public elapsed: (earlier: instant, later: instant) -> (span: duration) =
    span = duration_value(u64(later) - u64(earlier))
end elapsed

--- The instant `span` after `at`, a deadline; it traps past the clock's
--- last instant, 584 years after its start.
public later: (at: instant, span: duration) -> (result: instant) =
    result = instant_value(u64(at) + u64(span))
end later

--- The whole milliseconds in `span`, rounded down.
public in_milliseconds: (span: duration) -> (count: u64) =
    count = u64(span) / 1000000
end in_milliseconds
